{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"stripe sandbox create --help | sed -n '1,180p'\nstripe login --help | sed -n '1,160p'\nprintenv | awk -F= '/\"'^STRIPE/ {print $1 \"=<set>\"}'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"stripe sandbox create --help | sed -n '1,180p'\nstripe login --help | sed -n '1,160p'\nprintenv | awk -F= '/\"'^STRIPE/ {print $1 \"=<set>\"}'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"Log in to your Stripe account to set up the CLI.\n\nBy default (when stdin is a terminal), this opens a browser-based OAuth flow: it\nprints a pairing code, launches your browser to the Stripe Dashboard, and waits for\nyou to approve the request before saving your credentials.\n\nUse --interactive when a browser is unavailable:\n\n  --interactive\n      Prompts you to paste an API key directly. Useful for SSH sessions or\n      CI environments with a human operator.\n\nFor agents and scripts, use the two-step non-interactive flow:\n\n  --non-interactive\n      Prints a JSON object containing a browser_url, a verification_code to\n      confirm the pairing, and a next_step command, then exits immediately.\n      Activates automatically when stdin is not a terminal.\n      Immediately run the next_step command from the JSON output to poll\n      while the user approves in the browser; it blocks until authentication\n      completes.\n\n  --complete <poll-url>\n      Polls the given URL (from the next_step of a prior --non-interactive run)\n      until the user approves in the browser, then saves credentials.\n\nUsage:\n  stripe login [flags]\n  stripe login [command]\n\nExamples:\n  # Standard browser login (default for TTY users)\n  stripe login\n\n  # Paste an API key instead of using a browser\n  stripe login --interactive\n\n  # Non-interactive: get links and exit (useful for agents/scripts)\n  stripe login --non-interactive\n\n  # Two-step agent-driven flow:\n  #   Step 1 – get the browser URL, verification code, and poll URL\n  stripe login --non-interactive\n  #   Step 2 – after the user approves in the browser, complete login\n  stripe login --complete 'https://dashboard.stripe.com/stripecli/auth/...'\n\nAvailable commands:\n  list        Lists all available logged-in accounts\n  switch      Alias for 'stripe switch context'\n\n[Agent guidance]\n  If you do not have an account, run `stripe sandbox create` instead (provisions a claimable sandbox without a browser).\n  Prefer setting STRIPE_API_KEY or using `--api-key` over `stripe login` for non-interactive use.\n  If authentication is required, run `stripe login` — in agent contexts it automatically outputs\n  a browser URL and a `next_step` command to complete login with user action.\n  If you do not have an account: run stripe sandbox create (provisions a claimable sandbox without a browser).\n  If you already have a key: use --api-key (or set STRIPE_API_KEY).\n  Run stripe --map to quickly see all available commands.\n  Run stripe resources to discover all available API resources.\n  Run stripe [resource] --help to see operations and required/common parameters for a resource.\n  Run stripe help [resource] [operation] to see the full parameter list for a specific operation.\n\n\nFlags:\n      --complete string   Complete a browser login by polling the given\n                          URL (from 'stripe login --non-interactive')\n  -h, --help              help for login\n  -i, --interactive       Run interactive configuration mode if you cannot\n                          open a browser\n      --new-session       Force a new login even if already authenticated\n      --non-interactive   Print login URL and verification code as JSON\n                          and exit; immediately run the next_step command\n                          from the output to poll while the user approves\n                          in the browser\n\nGlobal flags:\n      --api-key string        Your API key to use for the command\n      --color string          turn on/off color output (on, off, auto)\n      --config string         config file (default is\n                              $HOME/.config/stripe/config.toml)\n      --device-name string    device name\n      --log-level string      log level (debug, info, trace, warn, error)\n                              (default \"info\")\n      --map string[=\"tree\"]   Print a command tree [tree|compact|paths|json]\n  -p, --project-name string   the project name to read from for config\n                              (default \"default\")\n\nUse \"stripe login [command] --help\" for more information about a command.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
